><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>We have presented evidence to suggest that decreased EA&#xD;and CHO availability may actually be a confounding factor&#xD;in a significant number of training-overload/OTS studies&#xD;(Table </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#0000FF;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>), resulting in misdiagnoses of training-overload/&#xD;OTS, instead of LEA leading to RED-S.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
